1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,001
December 2008.

2
00:00:01,001 --> 00:00:05,646
A fishing boat slips out of a small harbor
on Korea's west coast.

3
00:00:05,646 --> 00:00:11,292
According to investigators, one of the
passengers is the most wanted man in the

4
00:00:11,292 --> 00:00:11,864
country.

5
00:00:12,414 --> 00:00:18,517
Behind him, tens of thousands of people
are discovering that their savings are

6
00:00:18,517 --> 00:00:18,908
gone.

7
00:00:18,908 --> 00:00:25,010
Retirees. Homemakers. Small shop owners.
Families who had been promised that a

8
00:00:25,010 --> 00:00:29,235
simple health machine would pay them every
single day.

9
00:00:30,235 --> 00:00:35,359
Three years later, the news arrived from
China. He was dead. Heart attack.

10
00:00:35,359 --> 00:00:39,307
There was a death certificate. There was a
funeral video.

11
00:00:39,307 --> 00:00:41,384
And almost nobody believed it.

12
00:00:41,634 --> 00:00:46,958
This is the story of Cho Hee-pal. How the
scheme worked, who let it run, and why a

13
00:00:46,958 --> 00:00:49,490
country still argues about one funeral.

14
00:00:50,990 --> 00:00:55,827
The story begins in Daegu, a big
industrial city in South Korea's

15
00:00:55,827 --> 00:00:56,571
southeast.

16
00:00:56,571 --> 00:01:01,483
Daegu is known as a conservative,
tight-knit place. Neighbors know

17
00:01:01,483 --> 00:01:03,939
neighbors. Word of mouth matters.

18
00:01:03,939 --> 00:01:06,618
That would turn out to be important.

19
00:01:07,168 --> 00:01:13,110
The man at the center of this story was
Cho Hee-pal, born in 1957.

20
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:17,797
Korean media, citing investigators,
described him as someone with only an

21
00:01:17,797 --> 00:01:22,356
elementary-school education, who
reportedly worked as a laborer and drifted

22
00:01:22,356 --> 00:01:27,219
through small businesses, and who learned
the multi-level marketing trade in his

23
00:01:27,219 --> 00:01:27,705
forties.

24
00:01:27,955 --> 00:01:32,370
In October 2004, he set up a company in
Daegu called BMC.

25
00:01:32,370 --> 00:01:37,094
And it started selling a deal that sounded
almost too simple.

26
00:01:37,644 --> 00:01:42,967
Korea is famous for its public bathhouses
and saunas, called jjimjilbang.

27
00:01:42,967 --> 00:01:44,790
The pitch went like this.

28
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:50,271
You buy a health device. A massage
machine, a pelvic-correction bed, even a

29
00:01:50,271 --> 00:01:56,130
karaoke machine. One unit cost 4.4 million
won, roughly four thousand dollars at the

30
00:01:56,130 --> 00:01:56,478
time.

31
00:01:56,478 --> 00:02:01,779
You never take it home. The company rents
your machine out to bathhouses and

32
00:02:01,779 --> 00:02:02,337
clinics.

33
00:02:02,587 --> 00:02:04,368
And you get paid every day.

34
00:02:04,918 --> 00:02:09,411
Let's walk through it the way an investor
would have seen it.

35
00:02:09,411 --> 00:02:13,388
Step one. You pay 4.4 million won. That's
one machine.

36
00:02:13,938 --> 00:02:20,072
Step two. According to Korean reporting
from the time, about 35,000 won lands in

37
00:02:20,072 --> 00:02:26,206
your account almost every business day.
That's around thirty dollars, every day.

38
00:02:26,756 --> 00:02:32,814
Step three. After about 166 payments,
roughly eight months, you've received

39
00:02:32,814 --> 00:02:34,590
about 5.8 million won.

40
00:02:34,590 --> 00:02:38,225
Your money back, plus 1.4 million won on
top.

41
00:02:38,775 --> 00:02:43,265
The court that later tried the group's
number two described the promise as a

42
00:02:43,265 --> 00:02:44,683
fixed 35 percent a year.

43
00:02:44,683 --> 00:02:49,528
Run the numbers on eight months, and it
works out closer to 48 percent annualized.

44
00:02:49,528 --> 00:02:53,428
A normal Korean bank deposit at the time
paid around five percent.

45
00:02:54,428 --> 00:02:59,153
Korea actually has a word, and a whole
law, for this kind of business.

46
00:02:59,153 --> 00:03:03,135
It's called yusa-susin. Roughly:
unlicensed deposit-taking.

47
00:03:03,135 --> 00:03:08,805
It means collecting money from the public
and promising fixed returns, without being

48
00:03:08,805 --> 00:03:11,572
a bank, and without any license to do it.

49
00:03:11,822 --> 00:03:17,200
It's illegal in Korea for one simple
reason. It is the classic shape of a Ponzi

50
00:03:17,200 --> 00:03:17,677
scheme.

51
00:03:18,877 --> 00:03:21,775
The daily payment was the hook. The ladder
was the engine.

52
00:03:22,025 --> 00:03:27,786
According to a December 2008 report by the
Korean news magazine Sisa IN, investors

53
00:03:27,786 --> 00:03:33,546
were organized into ranks, like a company.
Department head. Bureau chief. Division

54
00:03:33,546 --> 00:03:36,286
head. Group leader. Executive director.

55
00:03:36,836 --> 00:03:41,674
To become a department head, the report
said, you had to run two lines of

56
00:03:41,674 --> 00:03:46,114
investors below you, and each line had to
bring in twelve machines.

57
00:03:46,114 --> 00:03:50,687
Twenty-four machines. Around 100 million
won of other people's money.

58
00:03:50,937 --> 00:03:54,148
Only then did the company pay you a salary
and bonuses.

59
00:03:54,698 --> 00:03:59,811
To climb to the next rank, the requirement
reportedly jumped to 280 machines.

60
00:03:59,811 --> 00:04:02,400
Think about what that does to a person.

61
00:04:02,650 --> 00:04:07,836
You are no longer just an investor. You
are a recruiter. Your income depends on

62
00:04:07,836 --> 00:04:12,957
your sister, your old classmates, the
women from your church, the man who runs

63
00:04:12,957 --> 00:04:14,270
the shop downstairs.

64
00:04:14,820 --> 00:04:17,103
And the system had a second trick.

65
00:04:17,103 --> 00:04:21,939
According to the same report, the
operation kept changing its name. BMC.

66
00:04:21,939 --> 00:04:27,311
Then a series of new company names, one
after another, with different brands for

67
00:04:27,311 --> 00:04:28,520
different regions.

68
00:04:28,770 --> 00:04:32,969
Prosecutors later found that the group ran
around twenty companies in total.

69
00:04:32,969 --> 00:04:36,781
When one name drew attention, the money
simply moved to the next one.

70
00:04:37,331 --> 00:04:40,640
But there was a basic problem underneath
all of it.

71
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:44,663
Machines rented to saunas could never earn
that kind of money.

72
00:04:44,663 --> 00:04:49,595
According to prosecutors, the "rental
income" being paid out was largely new

73
00:04:49,595 --> 00:04:52,385
investors' money, recycled to earlier
ones.

74
00:04:52,635 --> 00:04:57,662
When the medical device story wore thin,
the pitch reportedly shifted to real

75
00:04:57,662 --> 00:04:59,882
estate and redevelopment projects.

76
00:04:59,882 --> 00:05:02,493
The product changed. The promise didn't.

77
00:05:03,693 --> 00:05:05,587
The money did arrive. At first.

78
00:05:05,587 --> 00:05:10,474
Early investors were paid on time. They
told friends. They told family. And they

79
00:05:10,474 --> 00:05:12,918
earned commissions for bringing them in.

80
00:05:13,468 --> 00:05:18,969
From Daegu, the network spread down to
Busan and the southern coast, up through

81
00:05:18,969 --> 00:05:23,425
the Chungcheong provinces, and into Seoul,
Gyeonggi and Incheon.

82
00:05:23,425 --> 00:05:28,856
By late 2008, Korean media were counting
around fifty sales centers across the

83
00:05:28,856 --> 00:05:29,413
country.

84
00:05:29,963 --> 00:05:31,260
So who signed up?

85
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:34,311
Not bankers. Not professional investors.

86
00:05:34,561 --> 00:05:39,950
In the Seoul area, the same 2008 report
said, about seven in ten investors were

87
00:05:39,950 --> 00:05:45,612
homemakers in their forties and fifties.
Later reporting described large numbers of

88
00:05:45,612 --> 00:05:51,069
retirees, people who had lost jobs, and
families still recovering from the Asian

89
00:05:51,069 --> 00:05:53,456
financial crisis of the late 1990s.

90
00:05:53,706 --> 00:05:58,854
Some reportedly put in their retirement
money. Some borrowed to invest more.

91
00:05:59,854 --> 00:06:00,495
Why them?

92
00:06:00,495 --> 00:06:06,196
Think about where a middle-aged homemaker
or a new retiree stood in Korea in the

93
00:06:06,196 --> 00:06:06,908
mid-2000s.

94
00:06:06,908 --> 00:06:12,395
Interest rates were low. Pensions were
thin. Many had a lump sum, a severance

95
00:06:12,395 --> 00:06:17,311
payment or a matured insurance policy, and
nowhere obvious to put it.

96
00:06:17,561 --> 00:06:23,292
And the person making the pitch was rarely
a stranger. It was a friend from the

97
00:06:23,292 --> 00:06:28,371
neighborhood, or a relative, someone you
had trusted for twenty years.

98
00:06:28,921 --> 00:06:33,755
You put in your money. And then, every
single day, a payment lands in your

99
00:06:33,755 --> 00:06:35,322
account. Like clockwork.

100
00:06:35,322 --> 00:06:40,744
After a few weeks, the doubts fade. After
a few months, you're the one telling your

101
00:06:40,744 --> 00:06:42,377
sister she's missing out.

102
00:06:42,627 --> 00:06:47,929
That daily payment wasn't just a return.
It was proof. Or it looked like proof.

103
00:06:49,129 --> 00:06:51,588
Here's what makes this story hard to
watch.

104
00:06:51,588 --> 00:06:55,876
The scheme did not hide in the dark for
four years. The warnings are on the

105
00:06:55,876 --> 00:06:56,276
record.

106
00:06:56,826 --> 00:07:02,475
According to police and prosecutors,
police began looking into the group as

107
00:07:02,475 --> 00:07:03,530
early as 2006.

108
00:07:03,530 --> 00:07:09,556
In May 2007, a police station in Miryang,
in South Gyeongsang Province, opened a

109
00:07:09,556 --> 00:07:13,322
formal investigation into companies linked
to Cho.

110
00:07:13,872 --> 00:07:19,080
And then something happened that a court
would later describe in detail.

111
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:24,721
In August 2007, Kang Tae-yong, Cho's
right-hand man, handed fifty million won,

112
00:07:24,721 --> 00:07:30,652
twice, to a police sergeant at the Daegu
police agency. A sergeant who was working

113
00:07:30,652 --> 00:07:31,809
on the Cho case.

114
00:07:32,059 --> 00:07:36,543
A district court later found that the
sergeant, in return, dragged out the

115
00:07:36,543 --> 00:07:40,240
investigation and gave the group
"investigative convenience."

116
00:07:40,490 --> 00:07:45,294
Prosecutors also alleged that, when police
asked for transaction records, the group

117
00:07:45,294 --> 00:07:50,098
handed over records from an old, defunct
company instead of the one actually taking

118
00:07:50,098 --> 00:07:50,677
the money.

119
00:07:51,227 --> 00:07:57,224
In April 2008, according to Sisa IN,
Korea's financial regulator, the Financial

120
00:07:57,224 --> 00:08:02,461
Supervisory Service, flagged two of the
group's companies for illegal

121
00:08:02,461 --> 00:08:07,319
deposit-taking, and notified police in
Daegu, Busan and Incheon.

122
00:08:07,569 --> 00:08:12,043
In September 2008, victims began filing
complaints.

123
00:08:12,043 --> 00:08:14,236
By then, it was too late.

124
00:08:15,436 --> 00:08:20,299
In the autumn of 2008, the global
financial crisis was shaking markets

125
00:08:20,299 --> 00:08:21,064
everywhere.

126
00:08:21,064 --> 00:08:24,399
In Korea, investigators were finally
closing in.

127
00:08:24,399 --> 00:08:28,984
In October 2008 the payments stopped, and
the companies collapsed.

128
00:08:29,234 --> 00:08:33,770
According to Korean reporting at the time,
the group's central computer systems were

129
00:08:33,770 --> 00:08:37,010
wiped and the accounts were emptied before
police got there.

130
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:39,571
Cho was put on the wanted list.

131
00:08:39,571 --> 00:08:44,891
Some victims reportedly photocopied the
wanted posters themselves and carried them

132
00:08:44,891 --> 00:08:46,708
to ports around the country.

133
00:08:46,958 --> 00:08:52,263
But according to investigators, on
December 9th, 2008, he boarded a fishing

134
00:08:52,263 --> 00:08:55,871
boat from a small port in Taean, on the
west coast.

135
00:08:55,871 --> 00:09:00,822
At sea, he reportedly switched to another
vessel and crossed to China.

136
00:09:01,372 --> 00:09:06,785
His right-hand man, Kang Tae-yong, had
already fled to China the month before.

137
00:09:06,785 --> 00:09:12,059
Kang was the group's administrative vice
president. He ran the money and the

138
00:09:12,059 --> 00:09:13,239
computer systems.

139
00:09:13,789 --> 00:09:19,007
Years later, prosecutors and the courts
put a number on what the group had

140
00:09:19,007 --> 00:09:19,712
collected.

141
00:09:19,712 --> 00:09:23,097
About 5 trillion won. Roughly 4 billion
dollars.

142
00:09:23,097 --> 00:09:24,930
From around 70,000 people.

143
00:09:25,480 --> 00:09:31,899
That 5 trillion figure counts money that
went round and round, as people reinvested

144
00:09:31,899 --> 00:09:33,136
their "profits."

145
00:09:33,136 --> 00:09:38,627
Prosecutors estimated the real,
unrecovered losses at about 840 billion

146
00:09:38,627 --> 00:09:41,102
won. Around 700 million dollars.

147
00:09:41,352 --> 00:09:45,752
And they found that the group had stashed
away about 290 billion won.

148
00:09:45,752 --> 00:09:50,534
Korean media started calling it the
biggest fraud since the founding of the

149
00:09:50,534 --> 00:09:50,980
nation.

150
00:09:51,980 --> 00:09:55,003
Before we go on, keep two things in mind.

151
00:09:55,003 --> 00:10:00,605
One: a cheque, handed over in a hotel
coffee shop, to a man whose job was to

152
00:10:00,605 --> 00:10:01,342
catch Cho.

153
00:10:01,342 --> 00:10:07,166
Two: a single piece of paper from China,
dated ten days before the death it was

154
00:10:07,166 --> 00:10:08,567
supposed to record.

155
00:10:08,817 --> 00:10:14,059
The first explains how he got away. The
second is why so many people still don't

156
00:10:14,059 --> 00:10:15,239
believe he's gone.

157
00:10:16,439 --> 00:10:20,755
Here's the part that turned a fraud case
into a national scandal.

158
00:10:20,755 --> 00:10:26,068
How did one man run a scheme this big, for
this long, and then simply sail away?

159
00:10:27,068 --> 00:10:31,455
We've already met the first one. The
police sergeant on the case.

160
00:10:31,455 --> 00:10:36,921
The court found he took 200 million won
from Kang in total. Two payments in 2007.

161
00:10:36,921 --> 00:10:42,455
And a third, on October 31st, 2008, at a
hotel in Daegu, while the group's offices

162
00:10:42,455 --> 00:10:43,805
were being searched.

163
00:10:44,055 --> 00:10:47,108
A district court sentenced him to eight
years in prison.

164
00:10:47,658 --> 00:10:49,439
The second was more senior.

165
00:10:49,439 --> 00:10:54,123
In October 2008, he headed the
violent-crime unit at the Daegu regional

166
00:10:54,123 --> 00:10:55,046
police agency.

167
00:10:55,296 --> 00:11:01,417
According to the courts, on October 30th,
2008, he met Cho in person, in the coffee

168
00:11:01,417 --> 00:11:07,243
shop of a hotel in Daegu, and received 900
million won as a bank cheque. That's

169
00:11:07,243 --> 00:11:09,308
around 800 thousand dollars.

170
00:11:09,558 --> 00:11:14,286
The courts found that he took the money in
return for investigation information.

171
00:11:14,286 --> 00:11:19,191
According to reporting on the ruling, he
sat face to face with a man his own agency

172
00:11:19,191 --> 00:11:21,260
was hunting, and let him walk away.

173
00:11:21,510 --> 00:11:26,610
He was sentenced to ten years at first,
reduced to nine on appeal. The Supreme

174
00:11:26,610 --> 00:11:28,964
Court confirmed it in December 2016.

175
00:11:29,514 --> 00:11:30,688
It went further.

176
00:11:30,688 --> 00:11:36,263
An official at the Daegu prosecutors'
office, who had worked on the Cho case

177
00:11:36,263 --> 00:11:41,692
itself, was convicted of taking bribes
over five years, from 2008 to 2013.

178
00:11:41,942 --> 00:11:46,864
Most of it, the court found, came from a
businessman who was managing the group's

179
00:11:46,864 --> 00:11:47,654
hidden money.

180
00:11:47,654 --> 00:11:52,090
In return, the official passed on
prosecution information and helped bury

181
00:11:52,090 --> 00:11:53,002
investigations.

182
00:11:53,252 --> 00:11:59,823
His sentence: nine years in prison and a
1.4 billion won fine, confirmed by the

183
00:11:59,823 --> 00:12:02,319
Supreme Court in October 2016.

184
00:12:02,869 --> 00:12:08,015
By the time the investigation was done,
prosecutors said eight police and

185
00:12:08,015 --> 00:12:11,047
prosecution officials had faced
punishment.

186
00:12:11,047 --> 00:12:13,937
For the victims, this changed everything.

187
00:12:13,937 --> 00:12:19,436
The people who were supposed to catch him,
some of them, had been paid by him.

188
00:12:20,636 --> 00:12:21,982
So where was Cho?

189
00:12:21,982 --> 00:12:27,840
According to investigators, he moved
around China, reportedly living under

190
00:12:27,840 --> 00:12:28,869
another name.

191
00:12:28,869 --> 00:12:33,144
Then, in May 2012, Korean police made an
announcement.

192
00:12:33,694 --> 00:12:39,556
Cho Hee-pal, they said, had died in China
in December 2011, of an acute heart

193
00:12:39,556 --> 00:12:40,089
attack.

194
00:12:40,089 --> 00:12:45,342
Police said they had found Chinese
emergency-care records and a death

195
00:12:45,342 --> 00:12:50,443
certificate while searching the homes of
his family and associates.

196
00:12:50,993 --> 00:12:55,831
They pointed to a video of his funeral,
reportedly filmed by family members.

197
00:12:55,831 --> 00:13:01,242
According to Korean reporting at the time,
he had been cremated in China within days,

198
00:13:01,242 --> 00:13:05,253
and his ashes brought back to Korea before
the end of December.

199
00:13:05,503 --> 00:13:08,958
And Korean authorities only found out
about it five months later.

200
00:13:09,958 --> 00:13:11,471
The public didn't buy it.

201
00:13:11,471 --> 00:13:14,013
Cremation meant there was no body to test.

202
00:13:14,013 --> 00:13:18,491
Even the police kept him on the wanted
list, saying the death had not been

203
00:13:18,491 --> 00:13:20,307
confirmed one hundred percent.

204
00:13:20,857 --> 00:13:22,895
Remember that piece of paper?

205
00:13:22,895 --> 00:13:28,517
In 2015, victims' groups pointed to a
Chinese cremation document that, according

206
00:13:28,517 --> 00:13:33,999
to Korean reporting, was issued ten days
before the date of death it recorded.

207
00:13:34,249 --> 00:13:39,280
They also noted that early reports and
later official accounts named different

208
00:13:39,280 --> 00:13:41,990
Chinese cities as the place where he died.

209
00:13:41,990 --> 00:13:47,021
A man who said he was a former aide
reportedly told Korean media he had spoken

210
00:13:47,021 --> 00:13:50,827
with Cho on the phone after the date of
his supposed death.

211
00:13:51,077 --> 00:13:56,576
There were rumors of plastic surgery, of a
new name, of sightings in China and even

212
00:13:56,576 --> 00:13:57,503
back in Korea.

213
00:13:58,053 --> 00:14:03,350
None of those rumors were ever proven. But
the questions were understandable.

214
00:14:03,350 --> 00:14:08,852
After all, this was a case where officials
had already been caught taking money.

215
00:14:08,852 --> 00:14:14,149
In 2015, Korean TV investigative programs
took the story up again, and public

216
00:14:14,149 --> 00:14:15,112
pressure grew.

217
00:14:15,662 --> 00:14:21,019
Then, on October 10th, 2015, Chinese
police arrested Kang Tae-yong, the

218
00:14:21,019 --> 00:14:23,735
right-hand man, in the city of Wuxi.

219
00:14:23,735 --> 00:14:27,130
On December 16th, he was flown back to
Korea.

220
00:14:27,130 --> 00:14:33,015
Arriving at the prosecutors' office in
Daegu, he was asked by reporters if Cho

221
00:14:33,015 --> 00:14:33,770
was alive.

222
00:14:34,020 --> 00:14:36,609
He said he had seen Cho dead with his own
eyes.

223
00:14:37,159 --> 00:14:42,489
In June 2016, after a two-year
reinvestigation, prosecutors announced

224
00:14:42,489 --> 00:14:43,803
their conclusion.

225
00:14:43,803 --> 00:14:50,137
They said Cho collapsed after a night of
drinking at a hotel in Weihai, in China's

226
00:14:50,137 --> 00:14:56,626
Shandong Province, and died at a hospital
in the early hours of December 19th, 2011.

227
00:14:56,876 --> 00:15:01,819
They said they had consistent testimony
from people who were with him, and from

228
00:15:01,819 --> 00:15:03,759
fourteen people at the funeral.

229
00:15:03,759 --> 00:15:09,015
They said a DNA test on hair matched Cho,
and that the funeral video showed no signs

230
00:15:09,015 --> 00:15:09,703
of editing.

231
00:15:09,953 --> 00:15:12,940
They said they had found no evidence that
he was alive.

232
00:15:13,490 --> 00:15:18,514
Because he was dead, they said, he could
not be charged. The case against him was

233
00:15:18,514 --> 00:15:18,949
closed.

234
00:15:18,949 --> 00:15:23,291
Some victims' groups rejected that
conclusion and said they would keep

235
00:15:23,291 --> 00:15:23,911
searching.

236
00:15:24,161 --> 00:15:29,121
They argued that a strand of hair proves
who it came from, not whether that person

237
00:15:29,121 --> 00:15:30,028
is still alive.

238
00:15:31,028 --> 00:15:32,636
So, is Cho Hee-pal dead?

239
00:15:32,636 --> 00:15:36,053
The official answer from Korean
authorities is yes.

240
00:15:36,053 --> 00:15:41,548
The doubts have never been proven. But for
many victims, they have never gone away

241
00:15:41,548 --> 00:15:42,017
either.

242
00:15:43,217 --> 00:15:48,148
With Cho officially dead, Kang Tae-yong
became the highest-ranking member of the

243
00:15:48,148 --> 00:15:49,751
group ever to stand trial.

244
00:15:49,751 --> 00:15:52,032
Prosecutors asked for life in prison.

245
00:15:52,582 --> 00:15:59,258
In January 2017, a Daegu court found that,
from June 2006 to October 2008, together

246
00:15:59,258 --> 00:16:05,290
with Cho, he had collected around 5
trillion won from roughly 70,000 people

247
00:16:05,290 --> 00:16:11,000
through unlicensed deposit-taking,
promising a fixed 35 percent a year.

248
00:16:11,250 --> 00:16:16,085
It convicted him of fraud, embezzlement,
bribery, and hiding criminal proceeds.

249
00:16:16,085 --> 00:16:20,554
The court also found that he had paid the
police sergeant on the case for

250
00:16:20,554 --> 00:16:22,145
investigation information.

251
00:16:22,395 --> 00:16:28,054
And it found that, in China, he had worked
with organized-crime figures to try to

252
00:16:28,054 --> 00:16:30,709
collect money he believed he was owed.

253
00:16:30,709 --> 00:16:36,019
The judges called it a property crime
almost without precedent, one that had

254
00:16:36,019 --> 00:16:37,346
destroyed families.

255
00:16:37,346 --> 00:16:39,372
He was sentenced to 22 years.

256
00:16:39,922 --> 00:16:45,396
On appeal, his lawyers argued the sentence
was too harsh, and that some of the

257
00:16:45,396 --> 00:16:47,641
charges were really Cho's doing.

258
00:16:47,641 --> 00:16:53,325
The appeal court kept the 22 years. In
November 2017, the Supreme Court confirmed

259
00:16:53,325 --> 00:16:53,536
it.

260
00:16:54,086 --> 00:16:57,905
Prosecutors charged around seventy people
in total over the scheme.

261
00:16:57,905 --> 00:17:01,953
But the man whose name was on the whole
thing would never face a judge.

262
00:17:03,153 --> 00:17:08,969
That gap, a crime with no trial for the
man at the top, is part of why the story

263
00:17:08,969 --> 00:17:12,532
escaped the courtroom and moved into pop
culture.

264
00:17:12,782 --> 00:17:18,030
In December 2016, the Korean crime film
"Master" opened in theaters. It's about a

265
00:17:18,030 --> 00:17:23,213
smooth-talking chairman who runs a giant
investment fraud, and tries to fake his

266
00:17:23,213 --> 00:17:24,703
way out of the country.

267
00:17:24,703 --> 00:17:28,396
Its director said the villain was modeled
on Cho Hee-pal.

268
00:17:28,646 --> 00:17:32,025
More than seven million people saw it in
Korean cinemas.

269
00:17:32,575 --> 00:17:39,177
And the phrase Korean media had used, "the
biggest fraud since the founding of the

270
00:17:39,177 --> 00:17:40,385
nation," stuck.

271
00:17:40,385 --> 00:17:46,906
For a whole generation, Cho's name became
shorthand for a con man who never paid.

272
00:17:48,106 --> 00:17:50,214
But what about the victims?

273
00:17:50,214 --> 00:17:55,990
Prosecutors said about 95 billion won had
been tracked down and recovered.

274
00:17:55,990 --> 00:17:59,425
Against the losses, that's a small
fraction.

275
00:17:59,425 --> 00:18:05,670
Spread across 70,000 people, it's roughly
1.4 million won each. About a thousand

276
00:18:05,670 --> 00:18:06,294
dollars.

277
00:18:06,844 --> 00:18:09,737
And getting even that has taken years.

278
00:18:09,737 --> 00:18:15,448
About 71 billion won of the criminal
proceeds was deposited with a court in

279
00:18:15,448 --> 00:18:20,702
Daegu, split into four separate cases, to
be divided among creditors.

280
00:18:20,952 --> 00:18:27,320
The first payout came in December 2017: 32
billion won, to more than 17,000

281
00:18:27,320 --> 00:18:28,169
creditors.

282
00:18:28,169 --> 00:18:34,792
Then some victims challenged who should
count as a creditor, and how much each

283
00:18:34,792 --> 00:18:37,254
should get. Everything froze.

284
00:18:37,254 --> 00:18:40,820
Those lawsuits only ended in October 2025.

285
00:18:41,370 --> 00:18:47,888
On June 24th, 2026, the court in Daegu
restarted the payouts: another 32 billion

286
00:18:47,888 --> 00:18:53,672
won, to more than 22,000 creditors, with
money due to go out from July.

287
00:18:53,672 --> 00:19:00,027
Two smaller pots, of 5 billion and 2
billion won, are still waiting for dates.

288
00:19:00,277 --> 00:19:04,958
Korean media reported that more than
20,000 victims were represented by only

289
00:19:04,958 --> 00:19:09,147
around a hundred appointed
representatives, and that many had moved,

290
00:19:09,147 --> 00:19:14,260
changed numbers, or simply lost contact.
The court said it had to track people down

291
00:19:14,260 --> 00:19:16,539
through local administrative offices.

292
00:19:16,789 --> 00:19:20,418
Some, after eighteen years, will never see
the money at all.

293
00:19:21,418 --> 00:19:24,715
For many families, the damage was never
just financial.

294
00:19:24,715 --> 00:19:29,510
Korean media reported that some victims
lost homes, marriages, and their health.

295
00:19:29,510 --> 00:19:32,087
Many have spent nearly two decades
waiting.

296
00:19:33,287 --> 00:19:35,301
Here's the uncomfortable part.

297
00:19:35,301 --> 00:19:39,395
Cho's scheme collapsed in 2008. The idea
behind it never did.

298
00:19:39,945 --> 00:19:46,192
In February 2025, Korea's financial
regulator said it had received 410 reports

299
00:19:46,192 --> 00:19:52,438
about unlicensed deposit-taking in 2024,
up a quarter from the year before. It

300
00:19:52,438 --> 00:19:55,001
referred 35 companies to police.

301
00:19:55,251 --> 00:20:00,369
The packaging has changed. Instead of
massage chairs, it's new technology,

302
00:20:00,369 --> 00:20:02,236
crypto assets, real estate.

303
00:20:02,236 --> 00:20:03,758
But look at the shape.

304
00:20:04,308 --> 00:20:09,568
One. A fixed, guaranteed return, far above
what any bank pays.

305
00:20:09,568 --> 00:20:16,101
Two. Payments that come often, daily or
weekly, so the scheme feels real long

306
00:20:16,101 --> 00:20:18,223
before it can be checked.

307
00:20:18,223 --> 00:20:25,095
Three. A product story you can't easily
verify. A machine in a sauna you'll never

308
00:20:25,095 --> 00:20:25,604
visit.

309
00:20:25,854 --> 00:20:31,509
Four. Bonuses for bringing in others, so
victims become recruiters.

310
00:20:31,509 --> 00:20:38,178
And five. Time. Every month that nobody
stops it, the hole gets deeper, and the

311
00:20:38,178 --> 00:20:41,723
people inside it get more people involved.

312
00:20:42,723 --> 00:20:48,094
None of this is unique to Korea. Versions
of the same shape have turned up across

313
00:20:48,094 --> 00:20:49,818
Asia and around the world.

314
00:20:49,818 --> 00:20:54,990
That's not investment advice. It's a
pattern. And patterns are the one thing a

315
00:20:54,990 --> 00:20:56,581
Ponzi scheme can't hide.

316
00:20:57,781 --> 00:21:01,787
Officially, the story ended in a hotel
room in China in 2011.

317
00:21:01,787 --> 00:21:06,122
For many of the people who lost
everything, it never ended at all.

318
00:21:07,122 --> 00:21:10,612
The scheme didn't run on machines. It ran
on trust.

319
00:21:10,612 --> 00:21:15,060
Trust in a daily payment. Trust in the
friend who brought you in.

320
00:21:15,060 --> 00:21:19,782
And trust that if something was wrong, the
authorities would stop it.

321
00:21:20,332 --> 00:21:25,974
So here's the question to ask, every time:
if a business pays you more than any bank

322
00:21:25,974 --> 00:21:29,400
ever could, where is that money really
coming from?

323
00:21:29,950 --> 00:21:35,443
Next time: another country, another
promise that sounded safe. And another

324
00:21:35,443 --> 00:21:37,818
fortune that simply disappeared.

325
00:21:38,368 --> 00:21:40,172
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

326
00:21:40,172 --> 00:21:45,118
And tell me in the comments: have you ever
been offered a deal that paid every single

327
00:21:45,118 --> 00:21:45,351
day?

